---
/**
 * Korean typography corrections.
 *
 * The v2 design was built for Latin text and every rule in it assumes Latin.
 * Dropping Hangul into those rules produces four specific, visible failures.
 * This fixes them without touching a single English page: everything is scoped
 * to `:lang(ko)`, which matches `<html lang="ko-KR">` (CSS language matching is
 * prefix-based) and therefore never applies on the English site.
 *
 * Include on any Korean page. Cheap to add, and the alternative is discovering
 * each of these one screenshot at a time.
 *
 * ── 1. Line breaking. THE important one. ──────────────────────────────────
 * Korean has spaces between 어절 (eojeol, word-phrases), but the CSS default
 * lets a browser break Hangul at ANY syllable. So "업그레이드하세요" wraps as
 * "업그레이드하세 / 요." — a single word split across lines with an orphaned
 * syllable. To a Korean reader that is not a subtle kerning nit; it reads as
 * broken. `word-break: keep-all` restricts breaks to spaces, which is the
 * correct behaviour and what every Korean site sets.
 *
 * There was no `word-break` rule anywhere in this codebase before this file,
 * so every Korean line was breaking wrongly by default.
 *
 * ── 2. Fonts. ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 'InterV' and 'Nyght' are the site's own webfonts and neither contains Hangul,
 * so Korean silently falls through to whatever the OS picks. Naming the Korean
 * faces explicitly — AFTER the Latin ones — makes the fallback deliberate:
 * Latin still renders in Inter/Nyght, and only Hangul reaches the Korean face.
 * System fonts only, so this adds no network request.
 *
 * ── 3. Synthetic italic. ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * SplitHeadline's second line uses `.nyght`, a serif ITALIC. There is no
 * italic Hangul face, so the browser synthesises one by shearing the glyphs.
 * Sheared Hangul looks like a rendering error. Korean typography does not use
 * italic for emphasis at all, so the honest equivalent is upright.
 *
 * ── 4. Letter-spacing on the small-caps labels. ──────────────────────────
 * The design's label treatment is monospace + `letter-spacing:.14em` +
 * uppercase — right for a short Latin string, wrong for Hangul, whose syllable
 * blocks already carry their own visual rhythm. Tracked-out Hangul reads as
 * "포 함  사 항" — spaced-out characters rather than a word. Uppercase is kept:
 * it correctly capitalises embedded Latin ("Free의" → "FREE의") and is a no-op
 * on Hangul.
 */
---

<style is:global>
  /* Korean-capable system faces, listed after the Latin webfonts so Latin is
     unaffected and only Hangul falls through to them. */
  :lang(ko) {
    word-break: keep-all;
    overflow-wrap: break-word; /* safety valve: a single unbreakable run still wraps */
  }

  :lang(ko) body,
  :lang(ko) .lede,
  :lang(ko) .plan__who,
  :lang(ko) .plan__seat,
  :lang(ko) .plan__note,
  :lang(ko) .plan__feats li,
  :lang(ko) .ctable td,
  :lang(ko) .faq summary,
  :lang(ko) .faq p {
    font-family: 'InterV', Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont,
      'Apple SD Gothic Neo', 'Pretendard', 'Noto Sans KR', 'Malgun Gothic', sans-serif;
  }

  /* Serif display line — upright, not a sheared synthetic italic.
     BOTH selectors are needed and neither is redundant: the pricing page
     emphasises with `.nyght`, the homepage with a bare `em` carrying its own
     font-family and font-style:italic. `.nyght` does not occur ANYWHERE in
     homepage-v2-body.html, so targeting it alone meant this component was
     mounted on the Korean homepage and did nothing for its ~20 accent words —
     the page believed it was protected while every Hangul emphasis rendered as
     sheared synthetic italic in a font with no Korean glyphs at all. */
  :lang(ko) .nyght,
  :lang(ko) em,
  :lang(ko) .capsule__name {
    font-family: 'Nyght', 'Apple SD Gothic Neo', 'Noto Serif KR', 'Batang', serif;
    /* !important, deliberately, and this is the case it exists for.

       The design italicises `em` through at least six rules of varying
       specificity — `.foot__news h3 em` (0,1,2) already beats `:lang(ko) em`
       (0,1,1), and it was the one Hangul emphasis still shearing after the
       first fix. The alternative is enumerating every one of those selectors
       here, which breaks silently the next time the design adds a seventh.

       Synthetic italic on Hangul is not a style preference being overridden.
       There is no italic Hangul face, so the browser shears the glyphs, and
       sheared Hangul reads as a rendering fault. This has to win against
       arbitrary specificity we do not control. */
    font-style: normal !important;
  }

  /* The small-caps label treatment. Same list of selectors the design applies
     it to; tracking neutralised and a Hangul-bearing face named, since the mono
     stack has no Korean glyphs at all. */
  :lang(ko) .phero__note,
  :lang(ko) .bill .save,
  :lang(ko) .plan__badge,
  :lang(ko) .plan__inclab,
  :lang(ko) .ctable thead th,
  :lang(ko) .ctable .grp td,
  :lang(ko) .pfinal__note {
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Apple SD Gothic Neo',
      'Pretendard', 'Noto Sans KR', 'Malgun Gothic', sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  /* Headlines carry tight negative tracking tuned for Inter's Latin shapes;
     on Hangul that crowds the syllable blocks into each other. */
  :lang(ko) .h1,
  :lang(ko) .h2,
  :lang(ko) .plan__name {
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
</style>
